course: dictionary
lesson: 524
title: Astonished: surprised beyond belief
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Astonished means very surprised, so surprised that you can hardly believe what you see or hear. Look at the clues around it. «Alice was astonished to see a rabbit with a watch.» A rabbit with a watch is so strange that you would stop and stare, so astonished means much more than just noticing something.
explain: Astonished is the past form of the verb astonish. You can say «The news astonished us», which means the news surprised us very much. Astonish, astonishes, astonished and astonishing all come from the same word, and the -ing form, astonishing, describes the thing that causes the surprise.
explain: Writers choose astonished when they want a strong feeling. «He went off a little warm, but not at all astonished» tells you the character was not surprised at all, which is a clear contrast. In a story, astonished is stronger than surprised, so use it when the surprise is big.
fact: Astonished means very surprised, so much that you can hardly believe it.
fact: Astonished is the past form of the verb astonish: astonish, astonishes, astonished, astonishing.
fact: Astonished is stronger than surprised: a little surprise is surprised, a huge surprise is astonished.
fact: Common phrases are astonished at, astonished by and astonished to see, as in «She was astonished by the size of the cake».
fact: In science, results can astonish us when they go against what we expected.
exercise: «Alice was astonished to see a rabbit with a watch.» What does astonished mean here? | Bored and sleepy | Angry with the rabbit | Happy to be late | *Very surprised, so much she could hardly believe it
exercise: Which word is the past form of the verb astonish? | Astonishes | Astonish | Astonishing | *Astonished
exercise: In a science lesson, results can astonish us. Why might that be? | *Because they go against what we expected | Because they are the same as last time | Because scientists never write anything down | Because they are boring and easy to predict
test: Which word completes the phrase? «The children were astonished ___ the sudden noise.» | with | *by | to | about
test: In a story, which word shows a stronger surprise than surprised? | Hungry | Glum | *Astonished | Tired
exam: Mia says astonished is just a fancy word for a little surprised. What is the best reply? | *No, astonished means very surprised, much stronger than a little surprised | Yes, the two words mean exactly the same, so use either one | Yes, but only when you are sleepy | No, astonished means very angry
